// Expected: a0 at one session and at two.
//
// The session-sibling replay carve-out, reached through a MAC rather than the
// signature of session_replay_not_attack.vp, so the two exercise different
// rewrite paths into the same rule.
//
// At two sessions the attacker can hand Bob the pair (m#2, tag#2) drawn from
// the other session. Bob's ASSERT passes, because the MAC key is long-term
// and shared, and Bob ends up using a value the declared sender did not send
// *in this session*. That is still not an authentication attack: Alice --
// the identity the query names -- did honestly produce and send that exact
// value. Non-injective agreement holds, and Verifpal's authentication query
// is non-injective by design. Reporting a1 here would be a false attack.
//
// The distinction being pinned: `m` cannot be forged, only relocated. A model
// that wanted this to fail would need injective agreement, which is a
// different property and not one this query kind claims.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private mk
	generates m
	tag = MAC(mk, m)
]

Alice -> Bob: m, tag

principal Bob[
	knows private mk
	_ = ASSERT(tag, MAC(mk, m))?
	used_b = HASH(m, mk)
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: m
]
